// A growable array.
//
// `pop` and `last` answer `T?` rather than trapping, because unlike `a[i]` the caller usually does
// not already know there is one. `?` nests, so both are honest at a nullable `T`: `Vec<Node?>.pop()`
// answers `Node??`, where the outer absence is *the vec was empty* and the inner is *the element
// was null*. Nothing flattens them and there is no `Option` — see `vision/TECHNICAL.md`.
//
// **Seven of the shipped nineteen members, and one that is not shipped at all.** `core/map.wac`'s
// rewrite says *"only the surface a rewritten package reached for"* and this file did not, so a
// reader could not tell a dropped member from one that never existed. Twelve are absent —
// `withCapacity`, `fromArray`, `at`, `set`, `insert`, `remove`, `swapRemove`, `clear`, `first`,
// `isEmpty`, `capacity`, `extend`, `toArray` — none of them for a reason, all of them because
// nothing here needed one.
//
// **`get` was on that list until `@/packages/abi` wanted it, 2026-09-04**, and which caller wanted
// it is the interesting part. Nine loops here iterate a `Vec` and none of them index one, which is
// what `issues/lang/0322a` is about: a counter that exists only to dereference. The ABI decoder is
// the opposite case — its answer is *positional*, a caller asks for member 1 because the schema said
// member 1, and there is no traversal to write. So the first demand for `get` came from the one
// shape 0322a explicitly is not about, which is the argument for having both.
//
// ## `items()` is an addition, it carries `for … in` over every `Vec`, and it answers an open issue
//
// `gen<T> void items(const this)` has **no counterpart anywhere in the shipped tree**: `.items()`
// appears zero times in `packages/` and `core/`, and `core/vec.wac` has no iterator at all. Nine
// loops in seven vision files go through it.
//
// Which matters because `for … in` then means two different things by receiver. Counted over the 59
// rewritten files:
//
//     15  `for (T x in name)`          an array, or a variable holding a generator — native
//      6  `for (T x in call())`        a call answering a generator — native
//      9  `for (T x in v.items())`     a `Vec`, and only through the method below
//
// **A `Vec` is the one container that cannot be iterated directly**, and it is the container this
// tree uses everywhere. Nothing on any page says so.
//
// `issues/lang/open/0322a` asks exactly this and left it open:
//
// > **What it does on a `Vec`.** `Vec` has `get(i)` rather than `[]`, so either the desugaring knows
// > about `Vec` — which is a built-in knowing about a library type — or `Vec` grows whatever the
// > desugaring calls. The second is cleaner and is a decision rather than work.
//
// This file took the second and did not record it. It also took it with a **generator**, which the
// same issue scoped out — *"Nothing about generators, which the general form needs and this does
// not"* — so the answer here is the larger of the two shapes on offer, chosen by writing rather than
// by deciding.

export struct Vec<T> {
  T[] data;
  i32 len;

  Vec<T> create() { return Vec(T[](), 0); }

  i32 len(const this) { return this.len; }

  /**
   * The element at `i`, trapping outside the range — `a[i]`'s contract, since a caller indexing a
   * position usually knows there is one. That is the argument `pop` and `last` make in the other
   * direction at the top of this file.
   */
  T get(const this, i32 i) {
    if (i < 0 || i >= this.len) { trap "Vec.get out of range"; }
    return this.data[i];
  }

  /**
   * Grow if needed, then append.
   *
   * Growing is inside `push` and not beside it because an array needs a value to fill the new room
   * with, a `T` at a non-defaultable type has none, and the value being pushed is the one `T` that
   * is always to hand at exactly the moment growth is needed. A standalone `grow()` cannot be
   * written at all.
   */
  void push(this, T v) {
    if (this.len == this.data.len()) {
      i32 want = this.data.len() == 0 ? 8 : this.data.len() * 2;
      T[] bigger = T[want](fill: v);
      for (i32 i = 0; i < this.len; i++) { bigger[i] = this.data[i]; }
      this.data = bigger;
    }
    this.data[this.len] = v;
    this.len += 1;
  }

  /**
   * The last element, removed.
   *
   * The slot keeps its reference until a later `push` overwrites it, because a `T[]` at a
   * non-nullable `T` has no null to store — see `vision/QUESTIONS.md`, *How should a `Vec` drop its
   * reference to a popped element?*. Bounded by the high-water mark, but one popped root holds a
   * whole graph.
   */
  T? pop(this) {
    if (this.len == 0) { return null; }
    this.len -= 1;
    return this.data[this.len];
  }

  T? last(const this) {
    return this.len == 0 ? null : this.data[this.len - 1];
  }

  /** Every element in order. Writing an iterator is writing a loop. */
  gen<T> void items(const this) {
    for (i32 i = 0; i < this.len; i++) { yield this.data[i]; }
  }
}
